// On This Day. Every date of the year has its own entry, 29 February included,
// so the section is always genuinely about today — there is no fallback pool to
// fall through to, which is what used to cause repeats.
const HISTORY_BY_DATE = {
  // ---- January ----
  "01-01": { year: 1983, text: "ARPANET switched over to TCP/IP in a single 'flag day' cutover — the moment the internet as we know it began speaking its native language." },
  "01-02": { year: 1959, text: "Luna 1 became the first spacecraft to leave Earth's gravity, missing the Moon by around 6,000 km and ending up in orbit around the Sun, where it still is." },
  "01-03": { year: 2009, text: "The Bitcoin genesis block was mined, with a Times headline about bank bailouts embedded in it as a timestamp and a comment." },
  "01-04": { year: 2004, text: "The Spirit rover landed on Mars on a mission designed to last 90 days. It kept working for six years, and its twin Opportunity lasted fourteen." },
  "01-05": { year: 1933, text: "Construction began on the Golden Gate Bridge. The safety net strung beneath it saved 19 men, who called themselves the Halfway to Hell Club." },
  "01-06": { year: 1912, text: "Alfred Wegener first presented continental drift to a geological society. He had no mechanism to offer, and was dismissed for half a century until the sea floor was mapped." },
  "01-07": { year: 1610, text: "Galileo pointed his telescope at Jupiter and saw three points of light beside it. Within a week he understood they were moons — proof that not everything circles the Earth." },
  "01-08": { year: 1889, text: "Herman Hollerith patented a machine that counted punched cards electrically. It cut the US census down from years to months, and his company later became part of IBM." },
  "01-09": { year: 2007, text: "Steve Jobs introduced the iPhone at Macworld, demoing a device so unfinished the team had to follow an exact script to keep it from crashing on stage." },
  "01-10": { year: 1863, text: "The world's first underground railway opened in London, running steam locomotives through tunnels while passengers were assured the smoke was good for asthma." },
  "01-11": { year: 1922, text: "Insulin was first given to a diabetic patient, a 14-year-old boy in Toronto. Its discoverers sold the patent to the university for a dollar each." },
  "01-12": { year: 1888, text: "The Schoolhouse Blizzard swept the American plains on an unusually mild morning, catching thousands of people — many of them children walking home — with no warning." },
  "01-13": { year: 1888, text: "The National Geographic Society was founded by 33 explorers and scientists in Washington, who agreed to start a magazine nine months later." },
  "01-14": { year: 1943, text: "Roosevelt and Churchill opened the Casablanca Conference, where the Allies settled on demanding unconditional surrender." },
  "01-15": { year: 2001, text: "Wikipedia launched — originally just a side experiment to feed articles into a more traditional, expert-reviewed encyclopedia project." },
  "01-16": { year: 1969, text: "Soyuz 4 and Soyuz 5 performed the first docking of two crewed spacecraft. Transferring the crew meant spacewalking between them, as there was no internal hatch." },
  "01-17": { year: 1912, text: "Scott's party reached the South Pole and found Amundsen's tent and a polite note already waiting. 'Great God! this is an awful place,' he wrote." },
  "01-18": { year: 1911, text: "Eugene Ely landed a biplane on a wooden platform built over the deck of a moored warship, using sandbags and ropes as an arrestor system. Naval aviation started here." },
  "01-19": { year: 1983, text: "Apple launched the Lisa, the first personal computer sold with a graphical interface and a mouse. It cost $9,995 and flopped; almost 3,000 unsold units were later buried in a landfill." },
  "01-20": { year: 1892, text: "The first public game of basketball was played at a YMCA training school in Massachusetts. The baskets were peach crates, and someone had to fetch the ball out after every score." },
  "01-21": { year: 1954, text: "USS Nautilus, the first nuclear-powered submarine, was launched. It could stay under for months, limited by food rather than fuel or air." },
  "01-22": { year: 1984, text: "Apple's '1984' commercial ran during the Super Bowl. It aired nationally exactly once, and Apple's own board had tried to kill it." },
  "01-23": { year: 1960, text: "The bathyscaphe Trieste reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep, nearly 11 km down. The descent took almost five hours; a window cracked on the way." },
  "01-24": { year: 1984, text: "The Macintosh went on sale, the first mass-market computer built around a mouse, windows, and proportional typefaces." },
  "01-25": { year: 1995, text: "Russian radar detected what looked like a submarine-launched missile heading its way. The nuclear briefcase was opened before analysts worked out it was a Norwegian science rocket." },
  "01-26": { year: 1926, text: "John Logie Baird gave the first public demonstration of a working television, in a London attic, to members of the Royal Institution who queued on the stairs." },
  "01-27": { year: 1880, text: "Edison was granted the patent for the incandescent lamp. The real work was the filament: his team tested thousands of materials, including bamboo and human hair." },
  "01-28": { year: 1912, text: "Jackson Pollock was born. He would abandon the easel entirely, laying canvas on the floor and painting from above." },
  "01-29": { year: 1886, text: "Karl Benz patented the Motorwagen, a three-wheeler generally counted as the first true automobile. His wife Bertha proved it worked by driving 106 km without telling him." },
  "01-30": { year: 1969, text: "The Beatles played their last live performance on the roof of their London offices. The police arrived 42 minutes in, on a complaint about the noise." },
  "01-31": { year: 1958, text: "Explorer 1, America's first satellite, went up and immediately found something nobody expected: belts of trapped radiation circling the Earth." },

  // ---- February ----
  "02-01": { year: 1884, text: "The first instalment of the Oxford English Dictionary was published, covering A to Ant. It had taken 27 years, and the full first edition would take 44 more." },
  "02-02": { year: 1922, text: "Ulysses was published in Paris on Joyce's 40th birthday. It was banned as obscene in the United States for eleven years." },
  "02-03": { year: 1966, text: "Luna 9 made the first soft landing on another world and sent back panoramic photographs from the lunar surface, ending the fear that a lander would sink into the dust." },
  "02-04": { year: 2004, text: "Facebook launched from a Harvard dorm room, restricted at first to people with a harvard.edu email address." },
  "02-05": { year: 1971, text: "Apollo 14 landed in the Fra Mauro highlands. Alan Shepard smuggled a six-iron head aboard and hit two golf balls one-handed in a pressurised suit." },
  "02-06": { year: 1959, text: "Jack Kilby filed the patent for the integrated circuit. Robert Noyce filed a better version months later, and the two are credited jointly." },
  "02-07": { year: 1964, text: "The Beatles landed at JFK to a crowd of thousands. Two days later 73 million people watched them on the Ed Sullivan Show." },
  "02-08": { year: 1969, text: "The Allende meteorite broke up over Mexico, scattering two tonnes of fragments. Inside were grains of dust that formed before the solar system did." },
  "02-09": { year: 1969, text: "The Boeing 747 made its first flight. Its designers assumed supersonic travel would make it obsolete, so they put the cockpit upstairs to allow a nose that opened for cargo." },
  "02-10": { year: 1996, text: "Deep Blue beat Garry Kasparov in a single game — the first time a computer defeated a reigning world champion under tournament conditions. Kasparov won the match anyway." },
  "02-11": { year: 1990, text: "Nelson Mandela walked out of prison after 27 years, and the world watched live. Four years later he was president." },
  "02-12": { year: 1809, text: "Charles Darwin and Abraham Lincoln were born on the same day, on opposite sides of the Atlantic." },
  "02-13": { year: 1633, text: "Galileo arrived in Rome to face the Inquisition over his Dialogue. He was 68 and had to be carried much of the way by litter." },
  "02-14": { year: 1876, text: "Alexander Graham Bell filed his telephone patent, reportedly hours ahead of a rival filing on the same principle." },
  "02-15": { year: 1965, text: "Canada raised the maple leaf flag for the first time, after a parliamentary debate on the design that ran for six months." },
  "02-16": { year: 1978, text: "The first computerized bulletin board system went online in Chicago, built during a blizzard. Online community predates the web by more than a decade." },
  "02-17": { year: 1600, text: "Giordano Bruno was burned in Rome. Among his heresies was the claim that the stars were distant suns with worlds of their own." },
  "02-18": { year: 1930, text: "Clyde Tombaugh found Pluto by flicking between two photographic plates taken six nights apart and spotting a dot that moved. He was 24, and had no degree." },
  "02-19": { year: 1878, text: "Edison patented the phonograph. Even he underestimated it — his list of uses put recorded music fourth, behind dictation and talking clocks." },
  "02-20": { year: 1962, text: "John Glenn orbited the Earth three times in under five hours. A faulty sensor suggested his heat shield was loose, so controllers had him keep the retrorocket pack strapped on for re-entry." },
  "02-21": { year: 1947, text: "Edwin Land demonstrated instant photography to the Optical Society of America. The idea came from his three-year-old daughter asking why she couldn't see the picture now." },
  "02-22": { year: 1997, text: "Scientists in Edinburgh announced Dolly the sheep, cloned from an adult cell. She was named after Dolly Parton, for reasons the team was happy to explain." },
  "02-23": { year: 1455, text: "The traditional date of the Gutenberg Bible, the first substantial book printed with movable type in Europe. About 49 copies survive." },
  "02-24": { year: 1955, text: "Steve Jobs was born. He later credited a college calligraphy class he audited for the Mac's typography." },
  "02-25": { year: 1837, text: "Thomas Davenport received a patent for an electric motor. He was a blacksmith who had sold his wife's silk wedding dress for wire to wind the coils." },
  "02-26": { year: 1935, text: "Robert Watson-Watt showed that a BBC shortwave transmitter could reveal an aircraft flying through its beam. Britain had a radar chain along its coast within four years." },
  "02-27": { year: 1812, text: "Lord Byron gave his first speech in the House of Lords, defending the Luddites smashing looms. His daughter Ada would grow up to write the first computer program." },
  "02-28": { year: 1953, text: "Watson and Crick worked out the double helix and, by Crick's account, announced in a Cambridge pub that they had found the secret of life." },
  "02-29": { year: 1940, text: "Hattie McDaniel became the first Black performer to win an Academy Award. At the ceremony she was seated at a segregated table at the back of the room." },

  // ---- March ----
  "03-01": { year: 1896, text: "Henri Becquerel developed a photographic plate he had left in a drawer with uranium salts, expecting nothing. It was fogged. Radioactivity had been found by accident and bad weather." },
  "03-02": { year: 1972, text: "Pioneer 10 launched, the first spacecraft to cross the asteroid belt and reach Jupiter. It carries a gold plaque showing where it came from." },
  "03-03": { year: 1885, text: "American Telephone and Telegraph was incorporated to build the long-distance network. It would take 30 years to get a call from New York to San Francisco." },
  "03-04": { year: 1877, text: "Swan Lake premiered in Moscow to poor reviews and an orchestra that found it too difficult. It became the most performed ballet in the world after Tchaikovsky's death." },
  "03-05": { year: 1946, text: "Churchill, out of office, told an audience in Missouri that an iron curtain had descended across Europe. The phrase defined the next 45 years." },
  "03-06": { year: 1475, text: "Michelangelo was born. He always described himself as a sculptor, and considered the Sistine Chapel ceiling a distraction from that." },
  "03-07": { year: 1876, text: "Alexander Graham Bell was granted the telephone patent. Elisha Gray filed a caveat for a similar device the same day, and the two sides argued about it for decades." },
  "03-08": { year: 1618, text: "Kepler recorded the day he saw his third law of planetary motion — that the square of a planet's year is proportional to the cube of its distance." },
  "03-09": { year: 1959, text: "Barbie went on sale at the New York Toy Fair. Buyers were unenthusiastic; parents bought 300,000 in the first year anyway." },
  "03-10": { year: 1876, text: "Bell made the first telephone call, to his assistant in the next room: 'Mr Watson, come here, I want to see you.'" },
  "03-11": { year: 1918, text: "A cook at an army camp in Kansas reported sick with fever. It is among the first recorded cases of the 1918 influenza pandemic, which killed more people than the war." },
  "03-12": { year: 1989, text: "Tim Berners-Lee circulated a proposal at CERN for a hypertext system. His manager's note on the cover read: 'Vague but exciting.'" },
  "03-13": { year: 1781, text: "William Herschel found Uranus with a homemade telescope, the first planet discovered rather than simply known. He wanted to name it after King George III." },
  "03-14": { year: 1879, text: "Albert Einstein was born — on what would later, by coincidence of notation, also become Pi Day." },
  "03-15": { year: 1985, text: "symbolics.com was registered, the first .com domain in existence. Only five more followed that year." },
  "03-16": { year: 1926, text: "Robert Goddard launched the first liquid-fuelled rocket from his aunt's farm. It flew for two and a half seconds and landed in a cabbage patch 56 metres away." },
  "03-17": { year: 1958, text: "Vanguard 1 was launched. It stopped transmitting in 1964 but is still up there — the oldest human-made object in orbit." },
  "03-18": { year: 1965, text: "Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk, then found his suit had stiffened so much he couldn't fit back through the airlock. He bled air out of it until he could." },
  "03-19": { year: 1953, text: "The Academy Awards were televised for the first time. The producers had resisted for years, fearing nobody would go to the cinema." },
  "03-20": { year: 1916, text: "Einstein published the general theory of relativity. Its prediction that starlight bends around the Sun was confirmed during an eclipse three years later." },
  "03-21": { year: 2006, text: "The first tweet was posted: 'just setting up my twttr.' The 140-character limit existed to fit inside an SMS." },
  "03-22": { year: 1993, text: "Intel shipped the first Pentium chips. The name was chosen because you can't trademark a number, and rivals had been selling their own 486s." },
  "03-23": { year: 1989, text: "Two chemists announced they had achieved fusion in a jar at room temperature. Nobody could reproduce it, and 'cold fusion' became shorthand for announcing before you're sure." },
  "03-24": { year: 1882, text: "Robert Koch told a Berlin audience he had identified the bacterium causing tuberculosis, then the leading cause of death in Europe. The room was silent; nobody applauded." },
  "03-25": { year: 1655, text: "Christiaan Huygens discovered Titan. He also worked out what Saturn's odd shape was — a ring — and published it as an anagram to buy time to check." },
  "03-26": { year: 1953, text: "Jonas Salk announced a working polio vaccine on national radio. Asked who owned the patent, he replied: 'the people'." },
  "03-27": { year: 1964, text: "A magnitude 9.2 earthquake struck Alaska, the strongest ever recorded in North America. Parts of the coast rose 9 metres; others dropped by 2." },
  "03-28": { year: 1979, text: "A stuck valve and a misread indicator light led to a partial meltdown at Three Mile Island. No one was hurt, and American nuclear construction stopped for 30 years." },
  "03-29": { year: 1974, text: "Farmers digging a well near Xi'an hit fragments of terracotta. Beneath them were thousands of life-sized soldiers, buried for over 2,000 years." },
  "03-30": { year: 1853, text: "Vincent van Gogh was born. He didn't start painting seriously until his late twenties and was dead within a decade." },
  "03-31": { year: 1889, text: "The Eiffel Tower opened. Leading French artists had signed a public letter calling it a monstrous, useless iron chimney." },

  // ---- April ----
  "04-01": { year: 1976, text: "Apple Computer was founded. A third co-founder sold his 10% stake twelve days later for $800." },
  "04-02": { year: 1792, text: "The US Mint was established, and its first coins were struck from silver reportedly donated from George Washington's own household." },
  "04-03": { year: 1973, text: "Martin Cooper made the first handheld mobile phone call, standing on a Manhattan sidewalk — to his chief rival at Bell Labs, to tell him he'd done it first." },
  "04-04": { year: 1975, text: "Microsoft was founded to write a BASIC interpreter for a computer its founders had never actually seen in person." },
  "04-05": { year: 1722, text: "Jacob Roggeveen reached a remote Pacific island on Easter Sunday and named it accordingly. His crew counted hundreds of statues and could not work out how they had been moved." },
  "04-06": { year: 1896, text: "The first modern Olympic Games opened in Athens. Fourteen nations competed, and the marathon was invented for the occasion — there was no such race in antiquity." },
  "04-07": { year: 1969, text: "RFC 1 was published, a mild document titled 'Host Software' written by a graduate student who worried he was overstepping. The RFC series still runs today." },
  "04-08": { year: 1820, text: "A farmer on the island of Milos found a marble statue in a buried niche. The Venus de Milo reached the Louvre a year later, arms already missing." },
  "04-09": { year: 1959, text: "NASA introduced the Mercury Seven. All were military test pilots, and the selection process included swallowing a rubber tube and being sealed in a dark room for hours." },
  "04-10": { year: 1912, text: "The Titanic left Southampton on her maiden voyage. She carried lifeboats for about half the people aboard, which was more than regulations required." },
  "04-11": { year: 1970, text: "Apollo 13 launched toward the Moon. Two days out, a stirred oxygen tank exploded and the mission became a rescue." },
  "04-12": { year: 1961, text: "Yuri Gagarin became the first human in space, in a capsule flown almost entirely by automated control." },
  "04-13": { year: 1970, text: "'Houston, we've had a problem here.' Apollo 13's crew used the lunar module as a lifeboat and swung around the Moon to get home." },
  "04-14": { year: 1894, text: "The first Kinetoscope parlour opened in New York. For 25 cents you could peer into a cabinet and watch five short films, one at a time, alone." },
  "04-15": { year: 1452, text: "Leonardo da Vinci was born. He wrote his notebooks in mirror script and left most of his projects unfinished." },
  "04-16": { year: 1943, text: "A Swiss chemist absorbed a trace of a compound he was re-examining and had to be walked home. Three days later he took a deliberate dose and cycled home, in the interests of science." },
  "04-17": { year: 1970, text: "Apollo 13 splashed down safely after four days of improvised engineering, including a carbon dioxide scrubber built from a flight manual cover, a sock and duct tape." },
  "04-18": { year: 1906, text: "The San Francisco earthquake struck at 5:12 am. The shaking lasted under a minute; the fires that followed burned for three days and did most of the damage." },
  "04-19": { year: 1965, text: "Gordon Moore published a short article observing that the number of components on a chip had been doubling every year. He was asked to guess where it would go, and guessed right for 50 years." },
  "04-20": { year: 1902, text: "The Curies finished isolating a tenth of a gram of radium chloride, having processed tonnes of pitchblende by hand in a leaking shed." },
  "04-21": { year: 1989, text: "Nintendo released the Game Boy. It had a greenish screen and no colour, and it outsold every rival that had both." },
  "04-22": { year: 1970, text: "The first Earth Day brought an estimated 20 million Americans out in demonstrations. The Environmental Protection Agency was created before the year ended." },
  "04-23": { year: 2005, text: "The first YouTube video was uploaded: 19 seconds of a co-founder standing in front of the elephants at the San Diego Zoo." },
  "04-24": { year: 1990, text: "The Hubble Space Telescope launched. Its images came back blurred: the main mirror had been ground exactly to the wrong shape, and it took a servicing mission to fix." },
  "04-25": { year: 1953, text: "Watson and Crick's paper on the structure of DNA appeared in Nature. It ran to about 900 words and included one hand-drawn diagram by Odile Crick." },
  "04-26": { year: 1986, text: "Reactor 4 at Chernobyl exploded during a safety test. Sweden raised the alarm two days later after detecting fallout on workers' shoes." },
  "04-27": { year: 1981, text: "Xerox launched the Star, the first commercial machine with icons, folders, windows and a mouse. It cost $16,000 and sold poorly, and everything since has copied it." },
  "04-28": { year: 1947, text: "Thor Heyerdahl left Peru on a balsa raft to show that ancient people could have crossed the Pacific. The Kon-Tiki drifted 6,900 km in 101 days." },
  "04-29": { year: 1913, text: "Gideon Sundback patented the modern zipper. It sold slowly until the 1930s, when children's clothing was advertised on the grounds they could dress themselves." },
  "04-30": { year: 1993, text: "CERN released the World Wide Web software into the public domain, royalty-free. Arguably the single most valuable thing anyone has ever given away." },

  // ---- May ----
  "05-01": { year: 1931, text: "The Empire State Building opened, built in 410 days during the Depression. It stayed largely empty for years and was nicknamed the Empty State Building." },
  "05-02": { year: 1519, text: "Leonardo da Vinci died in France at 67. Among his possessions was an unfinished portrait of a Florentine merchant's wife that he had never delivered." },
  "05-03": { year: 1952, text: "The first scheduled jet airliner service took off, London to Johannesburg by Comet. Metal fatigue around the square windows grounded the fleet two years later." },
  "05-04": { year: 1953, text: "Hemingway won the Pulitzer for The Old Man and the Sea, a book he wrote in eight weeks after a decade of critics saying he was finished." },
  "05-05": { year: 1961, text: "Alan Shepard became the first American in space on a 15-minute suborbital hop, after a launch delay long enough that he had to use the suit rather than wait." },
  "05-06": { year: 1937, text: "The Hindenburg caught fire while landing in New Jersey, on live radio and newsreel film. Thirty-six died, and passenger airships ended that afternoon." },
  "05-07": { year: 1946, text: "Sony was founded in a bombed-out Tokyo department store with about twenty employees. Its first product was a rice cooker that didn't work." },
  "05-08": { year: 1886, text: "Coca-Cola went on sale at an Atlanta pharmacy as a patent medicine, five cents a glass. It sold about nine servings a day in its first year." },
  "05-09": { year: 1960, text: "The FDA approved the first oral contraceptive. Within five years a quarter of married American women under 45 had used it." },
  "05-10": { year: 1869, text: "A golden spike joined the American transcontinental railroad in Utah. It was wired to a telegraph so the hammer blow could be transmitted across the country live." },
  "05-11": { year: 1904, text: "Salvador Dalí was born. He claimed to induce his melting imagery by napping in a chair holding a key over a metal plate." },
  "05-12": { year: 1941, text: "Konrad Zuse presented the Z3 in Berlin, the first programmable fully automatic digital computer. It was built from telephone relays and destroyed in a bombing raid two years later." },
  "05-13": { year: 1888, text: "Brazil abolished slavery with a two-sentence law, the last country in the Americas to do so." },
  "05-14": { year: 1796, text: "Edward Jenner scratched cowpox into the arm of an eight-year-old, then exposed him to smallpox. The word vaccine comes from vacca, Latin for cow." },
  "05-15": { year: 1940, text: "Nylon stockings went on sale across America. Four million pairs sold in four days; two years later the same nylon was going into parachutes." },
  "05-16": { year: 1929, text: "The first Academy Awards were handed out in a 15-minute ceremony. The winners had been announced three months earlier." },
  "05-17": { year: 1902, text: "An archaeologist noticed a gear embedded in a corroded lump from a Greek shipwreck — the Antikythera mechanism, a 2,000-year-old geared computer." },
  "05-18": { year: 1980, text: "Mount St Helens erupted sideways after an earthquake collapsed its north face, removing 400 metres of mountain in minutes." },
  "05-19": { year: 1536, text: "Anne Boleyn was executed at the Tower of London. Henry had brought a swordsman over from France, considered a more reliable method than the axe." },
  "05-20": { year: 1927, text: "Lindbergh took off from New York for Paris alone, with no radio, no parachute and five sandwiches. He said he wasn't going to need more if he didn't make it." },
  "05-21": { year: 1927, text: "Lindbergh landed at Le Bourget 33 and a half hours later, to a crowd of 100,000 who dragged him off the aircraft and carried him around the field." },
  "05-22": { year: 1980, text: "Pac-Man was released in Japan, designed deliberately to appeal to players who had no interest in shooting things." },
  "05-23": { year: 1949, text: "The Federal Republic of Germany came into being under a Basic Law that its authors deliberately called provisional, expecting reunification. It is still in force." },
  "05-24": { year: 1844, text: "Samuel Morse sent 'What hath God wrought' over the first long-distance telegraph line, collapsing the time it took news to cross a continent." },
  "05-25": { year: 1977, text: "Star Wars opened on fewer than 40 screens. The effects work behind it created the company that later became Pixar." },
  "05-26": { year: 1897, text: "Dracula was published. Stoker's working title had been The Un-Dead, and his vampire was originally named Count Wampyr." },
  "05-27": { year: 1937, text: "The Golden Gate Bridge opened to pedestrians. About 200,000 people walked, ran and roller-skated across before cars were allowed the next day." },
  "05-28": { year: 1959, text: "Two monkeys, Able and Baker, flew to the edge of space and came back alive — the first primates to survive a US spaceflight and be recovered." },
  "05-29": { year: 1913, text: "The premiere of Stravinsky's The Rite of Spring in Paris set off a riot in the theater. It's now standard repertoire." },
  "05-30": { year: 1911, text: "The first Indianapolis 500 was won at an average of 74 mph, by a driver in a car fitted with a rear-view mirror so he could race without a riding mechanic." },
  "05-31": { year: 1859, text: "The great clock at Westminster started keeping time. Big Ben is the bell inside it, not the tower, which was renamed the Elizabeth Tower in 2012." },

  // ---- June ----
  "06-01": { year: 1967, text: "Sgt. Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band was released, with a collaged cover so elaborate the band had to get legal clearance for the faces on it." },
  "06-02": { year: 1953, text: "Elizabeth II's coronation was televised against the palace's wishes, and British television set ownership roughly doubled in the months around it." },
  "06-03": { year: 1965, text: "Ed White made the first American spacewalk and enjoyed it so much that getting back in was, he said, the saddest moment of his life." },
  "06-04": { year: 1783, text: "The Montgolfier brothers publicly flew an unmanned hot-air balloon in Annonay. The first passengers, three months later, were a sheep, a duck and a rooster." },
  "06-05": { year: 1977, text: "The Apple II went on sale, one of the first computers sold as a finished consumer product in a plastic case, with colour graphics and a keyboard attached." },
  "06-06": { year: 1944, text: "The Allies landed in Normandy. The invasion had been postponed a day for weather, on a forecast that gave one brief window in an otherwise impossible fortnight." },
  "06-07": { year: 1893, text: "A young lawyer with a valid ticket was thrown off a train at Pietermaritzburg for sitting in first class. Gandhi later called that night the turning point of his life." },
  "06-08": { year: 1955, text: "Tim Berners-Lee was born. He would later refuse to patent the web." },
  "06-09": { year: 1934, text: "Donald Duck made his first appearance in a Silly Symphonies cartoon, and immediately got more fan mail than Mickey." },
  "06-10": { year: 1943, text: "László Bíró patented the ballpoint pen. Britain's Royal Air Force became an early customer because fountain pens leaked at altitude." },
  "06-11": { year: 1770, text: "Captain Cook's Endeavour ran onto the Great Barrier Reef. The crew threw six cannon overboard to refloat her; two centuries later divers recovered them." },
  "06-12": { year: 1942, text: "Anne Frank was given a red-checked diary for her 13th birthday. The family went into hiding three weeks later." },
  "06-13": { year: 1983, text: "Pioneer 10 crossed the orbit of Neptune, becoming the first spacecraft to leave the region of the planets. Its last faint signal was heard in 2003." },
  "06-14": { year: 1951, text: "UNIVAC I was dedicated at the US Census Bureau, the first commercial computer built in America. A year later it predicted a presidential landslide and CBS didn't dare broadcast it." },
  "06-15": { year: 1215, text: "King John sealed Magna Carta at Runnymede under pressure from rebel barons. The Pope annulled it ten weeks later, and it became foundational anyway." },
  "06-16": { year: 1963, text: "Valentina Tereshkova became the first woman in space. She flew 48 orbits — more time in space than every American astronaut up to that point put together." },
  "06-17": { year: 1885, text: "The Statue of Liberty arrived in New York harbour in 214 crates. America had struggled to raise money for the pedestal until a newspaper campaign got 120,000 small donations." },
  "06-18": { year: 1178, text: "Monks at Canterbury described seeing the new Moon 'writhe' and throw out fire. Some astronomers think they witnessed the impact that made the crater Giordano Bruno." },
  "06-19": { year: 1978, text: "Garfield first appeared in 41 newspapers. Within a few years it was the most widely syndicated comic strip in the world." },
  "06-20": { year: 1837, text: "Victoria was woken at six in the morning to be told she was queen. She was 18, and her first act was to ask for an hour alone." },
  "06-21": { year: 1948, text: "The Manchester 'Baby' ran the first program ever executed from electronic memory. It took 52 minutes to find the highest factor of a number." },
  "06-22": { year: 1633, text: "Galileo knelt before the Inquisition and formally abandoned the claim that the Earth moves. He spent the remaining nine years of his life under house arrest." },
  "06-23": { year: 1912, text: "Alan Turing was born. His 1936 paper defined what a computer is before any computer existed." },
  "06-24": { year: 1947, text: "A pilot reported nine objects moving near Mount Rainier 'like a saucer skipping across water'. A newspaper compressed that into 'flying saucer' and the shape stuck." },
  "06-25": { year: 1978, text: "The rainbow flag flew for the first time, at San Francisco's Gay Freedom Day parade. The original had eight stripes; two were dropped because the dyes were hard to get." },
  "06-26": { year: 2000, text: "Two rival teams jointly announced a working draft of the human genome, having been persuaded to share a podium rather than race to a finish." },
  "06-27": { year: 1954, text: "The world's first nuclear power station to supply electricity to a grid started up at Obninsk, at five megawatts — enough for a small town." },
  "06-28": { year: 1914, text: "Archduke Franz Ferdinand was shot in Sarajevo after his driver took a wrong turn and stopped to reverse, directly in front of one of the conspirators." },
  "06-29": { year: 2007, text: "The first iPhone went on sale. It had no app store, no copy and paste and no 3G, and people queued for days." },
  "06-30": { year: 1908, text: "Something exploded a few kilometres above Siberia and flattened 2,000 square kilometres of forest. No crater was ever found, and the first expedition arrived 19 years later." },

  // ---- July ----
  "07-01": { year: 1979, text: "Sony released the Walkman. The first batch of 30,000 was expected to be hard to shift; executives thought nobody would buy a tape player that couldn't record." },
  "07-02": { year: 1937, text: "Amelia Earhart's last radio message came from near Howland Island, a target 2 km wide in the open Pacific. The search that followed was the largest in US naval history to that point." },
  "07-03": { year: 1886, text: "Karl Benz drove his Motorwagen through Mannheim in public for the first time. A local paper covered it; most readers were more interested in the noise than the machine." },
  "07-04": { year: 1997, text: "Mars Pathfinder landed and released Sojourner, the first rover to drive on another planet. It used airbags to bounce to a stop." },
  "07-05": { year: 1687, text: "Newton's Principia was published, laying out the laws of motion and universal gravitation. The Royal Society had spent its budget on a book about fish, so Halley paid for it himself." },
  "07-06": { year: 1907, text: "Frida Kahlo was born. She painted most of her self-portraits using a mirror mounted above her bed." },
  "07-07": { year: 1928, text: "A bakery in Chillicothe, Missouri, sold the first commercially sliced bread, using a machine other bakers had told the inventor nobody would want." },
  "07-08": { year: 1889, text: "The Wall Street Journal published its first issue, four pages long, grown out of a bulletin that Dow, Jones & Company had been hand-delivering to traders." },
  "07-09": { year: 1877, text: "The first Wimbledon championship began, to help the club pay for a broken roller. Twenty-two men entered and the final was delayed for the Eton–Harrow cricket match." },
  "07-10": { year: 1962, text: "Telstar relayed the first live television pictures across the Atlantic. It only worked for about 20 minutes per orbit, so broadcasts had to be scheduled around it." },
  "07-11": { year: 1979, text: "Skylab fell out of orbit and scattered debris across Western Australia. A shire council sent NASA a $400 fine for littering, which went unpaid for 30 years." },
  "07-12": { year: 1962, text: "The Rolling Stones played their first gig at the Marquee Club in London, billed as the Rollin' Stones, standing in for a band that had gone to a wedding." },
  "07-13": { year: 1985, text: "Live Aid ran from London and Philadelphia to an audience estimated at 1.5 billion. The satellite links held together on equipment borrowed from three continents." },
  "07-14": { year: 1789, text: "A crowd stormed the Bastille, a fortress prison that held seven inmates at the time, and took its gunpowder. The building was demolished within months and sold as souvenirs." },
  "07-15": { year: 2006, text: "Twitter opened to the public. It had been built in two weeks as a side project inside a struggling podcasting company." },
  "07-16": { year: 1945, text: "The Trinity test detonated the first nuclear weapon in the New Mexico desert. Physicists in the bunker had a betting pool on the yield; most guessed low." },
  "07-17": { year: 1955, text: "Disneyland opened to a press day so chaotic that staff called it Black Sunday: counterfeit tickets, a plumbers' strike and asphalt soft enough to swallow heels." },
  "07-18": { year: 64, text: "The Great Fire of Rome broke out among the shops around the Circus Maximus and burned for six days. The story that Nero fiddled comes from a historian writing 50 years later." },
  "07-19": { year: 1799, text: "French soldiers rebuilding a fort near Rashid pulled a black stone out of a wall. Its three scripts turned out to be the key to reading Egyptian hieroglyphs." },
  "07-20": { year: 1969, text: "Apollo 11 landed while its guidance computer threw overflow alarms. The software had been designed to shed low-priority tasks instead of crashing — and that's why they didn't abort." },
  "07-21": { year: 1969, text: "Armstrong stepped onto the Moon at 02:56 UTC, and Aldrin followed. They left behind a plaque reading 'We came in peace for all mankind' and a laser reflector still in use today." },
  "07-22": { year: 1933, text: "Wiley Post landed in New York having flown around the world alone in under eight days, guided by an early autopilot and a magnetic compass." },
  "07-23": { year: 1995, text: "Comet Hale–Bopp was found on the same night by two observers who didn't know each other — one at an observatory, one in a field with a friend's telescope." },
  "07-24": { year: 1969, text: "Apollo 11 splashed down in the Pacific. The crew spent the next 21 days in quarantine, in case they had brought anything back from the Moon." },
  "07-25": { year: 1978, text: "Louise Brown was born in Oldham, the first baby conceived by IVF. Her birth followed more than a hundred failed attempts and years of hostile press." },
  "07-26": { year: 1956, text: "The Andrea Doria sank eleven hours after a collision in fog off Nantucket. Both ships had radar; both crews misread it." },
  "07-27": { year: 1940, text: "Bugs Bunny made his official debut in 'A Wild Hare', chewing a carrot and asking what's up, doc — a line borrowed from a Texas schoolyard." },
  "07-28": { year: 1858, text: "A British administrator in India began taking handprints on contracts to stop people denying their own signatures. It was the first systematic use of fingerprints for identity." },
  "07-29": { year: 1958, text: "NASA was created by an act of Congress, folding in an existing aeronautics agency. It opened for business ten weeks later with 8,000 staff." },
  "07-30": { year: 1930, text: "The first football World Cup final was played in Montevideo. The teams couldn't agree whose ball to use, so they played one half with each." },
  "07-31": { year: 1971, text: "Apollo 15's crew drove the first car on the Moon, covering 27 km. Its top speed was about 13 km/h, and NASA insisted they never go further than they could walk back." },

  // ---- August ----
  "08-01": { year: 1981, text: "MTV launched. The first video it played was 'Video Killed the Radio Star.'" },
  "08-02": { year: 1939, text: "Einstein signed a letter to Roosevelt warning that a nuclear chain reaction might make an extremely powerful bomb. He later called it the one mistake of his life." },
  "08-03": { year: 1492, text: "Columbus sailed from Palos with three ships and a badly wrong estimate of the Earth's size. Had the Americas not been in the way, his crew would have starved." },
  "08-04": { year: 1693, text: "The traditional date of Dom Pérignon 'inventing' champagne. His abbey's own records show the opposite: he spent his career trying to stop the wine from fizzing, which was breaking the bottles." },
  "08-05": { year: 1858, text: "The first transatlantic telegraph cable was completed. It worked for three weeks, degrading until messages took hours a word, then failed entirely." },
  "08-06": { year: 1991, text: "Tim Berners-Lee posted a short public summary of the World Wide Web project to a Usenet newsgroup — the web's first public announcement." },
  "08-07": { year: 1974, text: "Philippe Petit walked a wire strung between the Twin Towers, 400 metres up, for 45 minutes. He was arrested on the roof; the charges were dropped for a free performance in Central Park." },
  "08-08": { year: 1786, text: "Mont Blanc was climbed for the first time by a doctor and a crystal hunter, chasing a prize offered 26 years earlier. Mountaineering as a sport starts here." },
  "08-09": { year: 1995, text: "Netscape went public without ever having turned a profit, and the price more than doubled on day one. The dot-com era had a starting gun." },
  "08-10": { year: 1675, text: "The foundation stone of the Royal Observatory at Greenwich was laid, to solve the problem of finding longitude at sea. Its meridian became the world's zero." },
  "08-11": { year: 1942, text: "Hedy Lamarr and George Antheil were granted a patent for frequency hopping, using a mechanism borrowed from player-piano rolls. The navy ignored it for decades." },
  "08-12": { year: 1981, text: "IBM introduced the Personal Computer. Its open architecture let competitors clone it, which IBM had not adequately planned for." },
  "08-13": { year: 1961, text: "East German troops began stringing barbed wire through Berlin overnight. People woke to find the city cut in two, with the wall arriving in the weeks that followed." },
  "08-14": { year: 1935, text: "Roosevelt signed the Social Security Act. He insisted on funding it through payroll contributions so that no future politician could easily take it away." },
  "08-15": { year: 1969, text: "Woodstock opened to far more people than had tickets. The organisers gave up on the fences, declared it a free festival and lost a fortune." },
  "08-16": { year: 1858, text: "Queen Victoria and President Buchanan exchanged greetings over the new Atlantic cable. Her 98-word message took about 16 hours to send." },
  "08-17": { year: 1877, text: "Asaph Hall found Phobos, six days after spotting Deimos. He had been ready to give up the search; his wife Angeline told him to try one more night." },
  "08-18": { year: 1868, text: "An unknown yellow line appeared in the Sun's spectrum during an eclipse. The element was named helium after the Greek for sun, and wasn't found on Earth for 27 years." },
  "08-19": { year: 1839, text: "France announced the daguerreotype process and gave it to the world free of charge — with the notable exception of Britain, where it stayed under patent." },
  "08-20": { year: 1977, text: "Voyager 2 launched, two weeks before Voyager 1, on a trajectory that would take it past all four outer planets. Both are still transmitting." },
  "08-21": { year: 1911, text: "The Mona Lisa was stolen from the Louvre by a museum worker who simply walked out with it under his smock. The theft is what made it famous." },
  "08-22": { year: 1864, text: "Twelve states signed the first Geneva Convention, agreeing that wounded soldiers and the people treating them should be protected regardless of side." },
  "08-23": { year: 1966, text: "Lunar Orbiter 1 took the first photograph of Earth from the vicinity of the Moon. The image was scanned onto film in orbit and radioed home line by line." },
  "08-24": { year: 79, text: "The traditional date for Vesuvius burying Pompeii and Herculaneum, though a scrawled charcoal date found in 2018 suggests the eruption came two months later." },
  "08-25": { year: 1835, text: "The New York Sun began publishing an account of bat-winged people living on the Moon, credited to a real astronomer who knew nothing about it. Circulation soared and no retraction was ever printed." },
  "08-26": { year: 1883, text: "Krakatoa's eruption reached its peak. The sound was heard 4,800 km away, and the dust cooled global temperatures and reddened sunsets for years." },
  "08-27": { year: 1859, text: "Edwin Drake struck oil at 21 metres in Titusville, Pennsylvania. His method — driving an iron pipe to keep the hole from collapsing — made drilling practical." },
  "08-28": { year: 1963, text: "Martin Luther King Jr spoke at the March on Washington. The passage everyone remembers wasn't in the prepared text — Mahalia Jackson called out 'tell them about the dream'." },
  "08-29": { year: 1949, text: "The Soviet Union tested its first atomic bomb, years earlier than Western intelligence had predicted. The US detected it from radioactive dust collected by a weather aircraft." },
  "08-30": { year: 1963, text: "The Moscow–Washington hotline went live. It was never a red telephone: the original was a teletype, chosen because writing avoids mistranslation under pressure." },
  "08-31": { year: 1897, text: "Edison patented the Kinetograph, the first practical movie camera. His studio, a tar-paper shed on a turntable, was rotated to follow the sun." },

  // ---- September ----
  "09-01": { year: 1985, text: "The wreck of the Titanic was found 3,800 metres down, 73 years after she sank. The search was cover for a classified navy survey of two lost submarines." },
  "09-02": { year: 1666, text: "The Great Fire of London started in a bakery on Pudding Lane. The Lord Mayor, woken and asked to authorise demolition to make a firebreak, said a woman could put it out." },
  "09-03": { year: 1976, text: "Viking 2 landed on Mars. Between them the two Viking landers ran biology experiments whose ambiguous results are still argued about." },
  "09-04": { year: 1998, text: "Google was incorporated in a garage. Its founders had earlier tried to sell the technology for about $1 million and found no buyer." },
  "09-05": { year: 1977, text: "Voyager 1 launched carrying a gold-plated record of greetings, whale song and Chuck Berry, with instructions on the cover for playing it." },
  "09-06": { year: 1522, text: "The Victoria reached Spain, the first ship to circle the world. Of roughly 270 men who set out three years earlier, 18 came back, and Magellan was not among them." },
  "09-07": { year: 1927, text: "Philo Farnsworth transmitted the first all-electronic television image, a single straight line, in a San Francisco loft. He was 21 and had sketched the idea as a farm boy watching plough furrows." },
  "09-08": { year: 1504, text: "Michelangelo's David was unveiled in Florence, carved from a block of marble two other sculptors had already given up on." },
  "09-09": { year: 1947, text: "Operators pulled a moth out of the Harvard Mark II and taped it into the logbook: 'First actual case of bug being found.'" },
  "09-10": { year: 2008, text: "The Large Hadron Collider circulated its first beam. Nine days later a faulty solder joint vented six tonnes of liquid helium and shut it down for over a year." },
  "09-11": { year: 1609, text: "Henry Hudson sailed into the harbour and river that now carry his name, looking for a passage to Asia and finding an island the local Lenape called Manhattan." },
  "09-12": { year: 1958, text: "Jack Kilby demonstrated the first working integrated circuit at Texas Instruments — a sliver of germanium with components wired together by hand, built while the plant was on holiday." },
  "09-13": { year: 1985, text: "Super Mario Bros was released in Japan. Its designers hid the whole game in 40 KB, reusing bushes as clouds by changing their colour." },
  "09-14": { year: 1959, text: "Luna 2 became the first human object to touch another world, by crashing into the Moon at 3 km per second and scattering steel pennants." },
  "09-15": { year: 1835, text: "The Beagle reached the Galápagos. Darwin didn't label which island his finches came from and had to reconstruct it later from the crew's collections." },
  "09-16": { year: 1620, text: "The Mayflower sailed from Plymouth with 102 passengers, having already turned back twice because her companion ship leaked." },
  "09-17": { year: 1908, text: "Thomas Selfridge became the first person killed in an aeroplane crash, on a demonstration flight with Orville Wright at the controls after a propeller split." },
  "09-18": { year: 1830, text: "A horse beat an early steam locomotive in a race in Maryland — the engine had built a lead before a belt slipped. The railroad still ordered engines, not horses." },
  "09-19": { year: 1982, text: "Scott Fahlman proposed :-) on a Carnegie Mellon message board to mark jokes. Every emoji descends from that suggestion." },
  "09-20": { year: 1519, text: "Magellan left Spain with five ships to find a western route to the Spice Islands, sailing on charts that showed a strait he had no proof existed." },
  "09-21": { year: 1937, text: "The Hobbit was published. Tolkien had written the first line on a blank page in a stack of exam scripts he was marking, without knowing what a hobbit was." },
  "09-22": { year: 1955, text: "ITV began broadcasting, ending the BBC's monopoly on British television. The first advertisement was for toothpaste." },
  "09-23": { year: 1846, text: "Neptune was found within a degree of where mathematics predicted, on the first night the astronomer bothered to look. It remains the only planet located by calculation before observation." },
  "09-24": { year: 1852, text: "Henri Giffard flew 27 km from Paris in a steam-powered airship, the first powered, controlled flight of any kind. The engine weighed 160 kg and produced three horsepower." },
  "09-25": { year: 1513, text: "Balboa waded into the Pacific and claimed it, and everything it touched, for Spain — the first European to see it from the American side." },
  "09-26": { year: 1983, text: "A Soviet early-warning system reported five American missiles inbound. The duty officer judged it a malfunction and did not pass it up the chain. It was sunlight on high cloud." },
  "09-27": { year: 1825, text: "The Stockton and Darlington opened, the first public railway to run steam locomotives. A man on horseback with a flag rode ahead of the train." },
  "09-28": { year: 1928, text: "Alexander Fleming came back from holiday to a stack of dirty petri dishes and noticed a mould killing the bacteria around it. It took another decade and a war to turn it into a drug." },
  "09-29": { year: 1954, text: "Twelve countries founded CERN, deliberately writing into its convention that all its results would be published openly." },
  "09-30": { year: 1882, text: "The world's first hydroelectric power station began operating on a river in Appleton, Wisconsin, lighting a paper mill and its owner's house." },

  // ---- October ----
  "10-01": { year: 1908, text: "The Model T went on sale at $850. Ford kept cutting the price as production improved, and by 1925 it was under $300." },
  "10-02": { year: 1836, text: "Darwin arrived home after five years on the Beagle. He never left Britain again, and spent the next twenty years working out what he had seen." },
  "10-03": { year: 1990, text: "East and West Germany reunified, eleven months after the Wall opened." },
  "10-04": { year: 1957, text: "Sputnik 1 launched. The panic it caused in the U.S. led directly to the agency that funded ARPANET." },
  "10-05": { year: 2018, text: "Banksy's Girl with Balloon shredded itself seconds after selling at Sotheby's, via a mechanism hidden in the frame for years." },
  "10-06": { year: 1927, text: "The Jazz Singer opened with synchronised dialogue. Within three years silent films were finished, and so were many of the careers built on them." },
  "10-07": { year: 1959, text: "Luna 3 sent back the first photographs of the far side of the Moon. The spacecraft developed film on board, scanned it, and radioed the images home." },
  "10-08": { year: 1871, text: "The Great Chicago Fire began in a barn on DeKoven Street. The reporter who blamed Mrs O'Leary's cow admitted decades later that he had made it up." },
  "10-09": { year: 1604, text: "Kepler's Supernova appeared in the sky, bright enough to see in daylight. It remains the last supernova observed in our own galaxy with the naked eye." },
  "10-10": { year: 1846, text: "Triton was discovered 17 days after Neptune itself, by a brewer with a private telescope who happened to be a very good observer." },
  "10-11": { year: 1968, text: "Apollo 7 launched, the first crewed Apollo flight, and sent the first live television from an American spacecraft. The crew all had colds and argued with mission control for eleven days." },
  "10-12": { year: 1979, text: "The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy was published, after Douglas Adams was locked in a hotel room by his editor to get him to finish it." },
  "10-13": { year: 1884, text: "An international conference adopted Greenwich as the prime meridian, by 22 votes to one. France abstained and kept using Paris time for another 27 years." },
  "10-14": { year: 1947, text: "Chuck Yeager broke the sound barrier with two broken ribs from a fall off a horse, having taped a broom handle to the hatch so he could close it one-armed." },
  "10-15": { year: 1997, text: "Cassini launched for Saturn on a seven-year trip that included flybys of Venus twice, Earth and Jupiter just to build up speed." },
  "10-16": { year: 1846, text: "A dentist demonstrated ether at Massachusetts General Hospital and a tumour was removed from a conscious-looking but insensible patient. The surgeon told the room: 'Gentlemen, this is no humbug.'" },
  "10-17": { year: 1931, text: "Al Capone was convicted of tax evasion. Prosecutors had failed to make anything else stick, so they used the fact that illegal income is still taxable." },
  "10-18": { year: 1922, text: "The BBC was founded as a company owned by radio manufacturers, who wanted programmes to exist so people would buy sets." },
  "10-19": { year: 2017, text: "Astronomers spotted 'Oumuamua, the first known object from another star to pass through the solar system. It was moving too fast to be bound to the Sun." },
  "10-20": { year: 1973, text: "The Sydney Opera House opened, ten years late and fourteen times over budget. Its architect had resigned seven years earlier and never saw the finished building." },
  "10-21": { year: 1879, text: "Edison's team tested a carbonised cotton thread filament that burned for over 13 hours. The trick wasn't the glow, it was the vacuum." },
  "10-22": { year: 1938, text: "Chester Carlson made the first xerographic copy in a rented room behind a beauty salon. It read '10-22-38 ASTORIA'. Twenty companies turned the invention down." },
  "10-23": { year: 2001, text: "Apple introduced the iPod. The press response was largely a shrug about an expensive music player with no wireless." },
  "10-24": { year: 1861, text: "The transcontinental telegraph was completed. The Pony Express, whose riders had been carrying mail across the same gap, announced it was shutting down two days later." },
  "10-25": { year: 1881, text: "Pablo Picasso was born. He was a fully trained academic realist by his teens and spent the rest of his life dismantling that training." },
  "10-26": { year: 1825, text: "The Erie Canal opened, cutting the cost of moving goods from Buffalo to New York by about 95% and turning New York into America's main port." },
  "10-27": { year: 1904, text: "The New York subway opened, 9.1 miles for a nickel. More than 100,000 people rode it on the first day." },
  "10-28": { year: 1955, text: "Bill Gates was born. He wrote his first program, a tic-tac-toe game, at thirteen." },
  "10-29": { year: 1969, text: "The first message was sent over ARPANET. They typed 'LOGIN,' the system crashed after two characters, and the internet's first word was 'LO.'" },
  "10-30": { year: 1938, text: "Orson Welles broadcast The War of the Worlds as simulated news bulletins — an early demonstration that the format of a medium can be more persuasive than its content." },
  "10-31": { year: 1517, text: "Luther is said to have posted 95 theses on a church door in Wittenberg. Whether he nailed them up or simply posted them to his archbishop, printers had them all over Germany within weeks." },

  // ---- November ----
  "11-01": { year: 1512, text: "The Sistine Chapel ceiling was revealed to the public after four years of work Michelangelo had tried to refuse." },
  "11-02": { year: 1988, text: "A graduate student released a worm to measure the size of the internet. A flaw made machines reinfect themselves until they seized up, and thousands of computers were paralysed overnight." },
  "11-03": { year: 1957, text: "Laika launched aboard Sputnik 2, the first animal to orbit the Earth. There was no plan to bring her back, a fact the programme's scientists later said they regretted." },
  "11-04": { year: 1922, text: "Howard Carter found the sealed entrance to Tutankhamun's tomb, preserving the largest intact collection of ancient Egyptian art ever recovered." },
  "11-05": { year: 1605, text: "Guy Fawkes was found in a cellar beneath the House of Lords with 36 barrels of gunpowder, enough to level the building and much of the street." },
  "11-06": { year: 1869, text: "Rutgers and Princeton played the first intercollegiate American football game, 25 players a side, under rules closer to football than to anything played today." },
  "11-07": { year: 1940, text: "The Tacoma Narrows Bridge twisted itself apart in a 40 mph wind, four months after opening. The film of it is still shown in first-year engineering courses." },
  "11-08": { year: 1895, text: "Wilhelm Röntgen noticed a screen glowing across the room from a covered tube. Weeks later he photographed his wife's hand; she said, 'I have seen my death.'" },
  "11-09": { year: 1989, text: "The Berlin Wall opened. Within days, sections of it were being cut up and sold as art objects." },
  "11-10": { year: 1969, text: "Sesame Street premiered, built on research into whether the techniques of advertising could be used to teach children the alphabet." },
  "11-11": { year: 1918, text: "The armistice took effect at 11 am. It had been signed at 5 that morning, and men were killed in the six hours in between." },
  "11-12": { year: 1970, text: "Oregon highway officials tried to dispose of a beached whale with half a tonne of dynamite. Blubber rained down for hundreds of metres and flattened a car in the car park." },
  "11-13": { year: 1907, text: "A French bicycle maker's helicopter lifted a pilot clear of the ground for about 20 seconds — the first free flight by a rotary-wing aircraft." },
  "11-14": { year: 1889, text: "Nellie Bly set off to beat the fictional record in Around the World in Eighty Days. She did it in 72, travelling with one dress and a small bag." },
  "11-15": { year: 1971, text: "Intel advertised the 4004, the first microprocessor sold commercially, as 'a new era in integrated electronics'. It ran at 740 kHz and was designed for a calculator." },
  "11-16": { year: 1974, text: "The Arecibo message was beamed at a star cluster 25,000 light years away. It was really a demonstration for the people at the ceremony; no reply is due for 50,000 years." },
  "11-17": { year: 1970, text: "Douglas Engelbart received the patent for the mouse, filed under the name 'X-Y position indicator for a display system'. He never earned a royalty from it." },
  "11-18": { year: 1928, text: "Steamboat Willie premiered — not the first Mickey Mouse cartoon, but the first with synchronized sound, which is why it's the one everyone remembers." },
  "11-19": { year: 1863, text: "Lincoln spoke for about two minutes at Gettysburg. The main speaker before him had gone on for two hours, and afterwards wrote that Lincoln had said more in two minutes than he had in two hours." },
  "11-20": { year: 1998, text: "The first module of the International Space Station launched. It has been continuously occupied since November 2000." },
  "11-21": { year: 1783, text: "Two men rose over Paris in a Montgolfier balloon, the first free flight with people aboard. They carried buckets of water and sponges in case the paper caught fire." },
  "11-22": { year: 1995, text: "Toy Story opened, the first feature film animated entirely by computer. Every frame was rendered from scratch by a farm of machines running for months." },
  "11-23": { year: 1963, text: "Doctor Who was first broadcast, the day after Kennedy's assassination. So few people saw it that the BBC repeated the episode the following week." },
  "11-24": { year: 1974, text: "A 3.2-million-year-old hominin skeleton was found in Ethiopia and nicknamed Lucy, after the Beatles song playing on a tape in the camp that night." },
  "11-25": { year: 1867, text: "Alfred Nobel patented dynamite, having found that nitroglycerin absorbed into porous earth could be handled safely. His brother had died in an explosion four years earlier." },
  "11-26": { year: 1922, text: "Howard Carter widened a hole in a sealed door and held a candle to it. Asked whether he could see anything, he said: 'Yes, wonderful things.'" },
  "11-27": { year: 1895, text: "Nobel signed the will that created the prizes. A French paper had mistakenly printed his obituary years earlier under the headline 'The merchant of death is dead'." },
  "11-28": { year: 1520, text: "Magellan's fleet emerged from the strait that now bears his name into an ocean so calm after weeks of storms that he called it the Pacific." },
  "11-29": { year: 1972, text: "Atari released Pong. The first cabinet, in a California bar, broke down within days because the coin box was jammed full of quarters." },
  "11-30": { year: 2022, text: "ChatGPT was released as a low-key research preview. It reached a million users in five days." },

  // ---- December ----
  "12-01": { year: 1913, text: "Ford's moving assembly line started at Highland Park, cutting chassis assembly from over 12 hours to around 90 minutes. Worker turnover was so high he had to double wages to keep anyone." },
  "12-02": { year: 1942, text: "The first self-sustaining nuclear chain reaction ran under the stands of a disused squash court in Chicago. The coded message sent afterwards: 'The Italian navigator has landed in the New World.'" },
  "12-03": { year: 1967, text: "The first human heart transplant was performed in Cape Town. The patient lived 18 days before dying of pneumonia, his immune system suppressed to stop rejection." },
  "12-04": { year: 1791, text: "The Observer published its first issue, and is still going — the world's oldest Sunday newspaper." },
  "12-05": { year: 1933, text: "Prohibition ended in the United States after thirteen years, having done rather more for organised crime than for temperance." },
  "12-06": { year: 1877, text: "Edison walked into the offices of Scientific American, put a small machine on the desk, turned a crank, and it asked the editors how they liked the phonograph." },
  "12-07": { year: 1972, text: "Apollo 17 launched, the last crewed flight to the Moon. On the way out the crew took the photograph of the whole Earth that became known as the Blue Marble." },
  "12-08": { year: 1993, text: "GPS was declared to have initial operational capability for civilian use. Until 2000 the civilian signal was deliberately degraded, giving accuracy of about 100 metres." },
  "12-09": { year: 1968, text: "Douglas Engelbart's 'Mother of All Demos' showed the mouse, hypertext, windows, video conferencing, and collaborative editing — in one 90-minute session, decades before any of it shipped." },
  "12-10": { year: 1815, text: "Ada Lovelace was born. Her notes on Babbage's Analytical Engine include the first published algorithm intended for a machine." },
  "12-11": { year: 1997, text: "The Kyoto Protocol was adopted, the first agreement to set binding emissions targets. It took another eight years to come into force." },
  "12-12": { year: 1913, text: "The Mona Lisa was recovered in Florence, two years after its theft, when the thief tried to sell it to an art dealer." },
  "12-13": { year: 1642, text: "Abel Tasman sighted the west coast of New Zealand. He never landed, and Europeans did not come back for 127 years." },
  "12-14": { year: 1911, text: "Amundsen reached the South Pole, having planned around dogs and skis while his rivals relied on ponies and motor sledges. His party got back without losing a man." },
  "12-15": { year: 1791, text: "The Bill of Rights was ratified — ten amendments out of twelve proposed. One of the two rejected was finally ratified in 1992, after 202 years." },
  "12-16": { year: 1947, text: "Bell Labs built the first working transistor. Nearly every device you have ever used contains descendants of it, now measured in the billions per chip." },
  "12-17": { year: 1903, text: "The Wright brothers flew. The longest flight that day covered 852 feet — shorter than the wingspan of a modern airliner's route to the gate." },
  "12-18": { year: 1958, text: "SCORE, the first communications satellite, broadcast a recorded Christmas message from President Eisenhower — the first human voice relayed from space." },
  "12-19": { year: 1972, text: "Apollo 17 splashed down. Nobody has been back to the Moon since, a gap now longer than the entire history of the space programme up to that point." },
  "12-20": { year: 1951, text: "An experimental reactor in the Idaho desert lit four light bulbs — the first useful electricity generated from nuclear fission. The engineers chalked their names on the wall beside them." },
  "12-21": { year: 1937, text: "Snow White premiered, the first full-length cel-animated feature. The industry had called it Disney's Folly." },
  "12-22": { year: 1894, text: "A French artillery officer was convicted of treason on forged evidence, and the affair split the country for a decade. Zola's open letter, 'J'accuse…!', ran on a front page four years later." },
  "12-23": { year: 1947, text: "Bell Labs demonstrated the transistor to management: a strip of gold foil on a plastic wedge pressed against germanium. The press release six months later ran on page 46 of the New York Times." },
  "12-24": { year: 1968, text: "Apollo 8 orbited the Moon on Christmas Eve, read from Genesis to the largest television audience yet assembled, and photographed the Earth rising over the lunar horizon." },
  "12-25": { year: 1990, text: "Tim Berners-Lee got the first web browser and web server talking to each other — the first successful client-server exchange over the World Wide Web." },
  "12-26": { year: 1898, text: "The Curies announced radium, isolated from pitchblende residues they had processed in a shed that Marie described as a cross between a stable and a potato cellar." },
  "12-27": { year: 1831, text: "The Beagle sailed from Plymouth with a 22-year-old naturalist aboard whose father had thought the voyage a waste of time. The trip was meant to last two years and took five." },
  "12-28": { year: 1895, text: "The Lumière brothers held the first commercial film screening in a Paris basement. Thirty-three people paid a franc each to watch workers leave a factory." },
  "12-29": { year: 1170, text: "Thomas Becket was killed by four knights in Canterbury Cathedral, after Henry II asked aloud who would rid him of this turbulent priest." },
  "12-30": { year: 1922, text: "The Soviet Union was formally established, uniting four republics under a treaty that lasted until almost the day, 69 years later, when it dissolved." },
  "12-31": { year: 1999, text: "The world sat up to watch for Y2K failures. After an estimated $300 billion of remediation, the most notable problems were a few parking meters and a nuclear plant's monitoring display." }
};


// APPS — the pool for the App Duel game (duel.js). Players compare two
// products and pick the one with more users.
//
// domain  the company's own domain, used only to fetch its favicon as a logo
//         (see duel.js). Nothing is bundled; a card falls back to a monogram.
// users   the headline figure, as an integer
// metric  what that figure actually counts. These are NOT all the same thing —
//         monthly actives, registered accounts and paying subscribers measure
